WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.170 --> 00:00:14.540
The Civil Rights Movement in the United States. Even after slavery ended in 1865, African Americans in the United States still faced discrimination.

2
00:00:14.540 --> 00:00:21.540
The policy of segregation kept them separate from whites. It was hard for African Americans to vote.

3
00:00:21.540 --> 00:00:30.540
They couldn't use the same restaurants, hotels, pools, or train cars as whites. They were even forced to sit at the back of the bus.

4
00:00:31.540 --> 00:00:40.540
People knew that this wasn't right, so they started fighting for racial equality under the law. Their efforts came to be known as the Civil Rights Movement.

5
00:00:40.540 --> 00:00:46.890
Many of the heroes of the Civil Rights Movement were just ordinary people.

6
00:00:46.890 --> 00:00:53.890
One was Rosa Parks, a black woman who refused to move to the back of a bus in Montgomery, Alabama.

7
00:00:54.890 --> 00:01:04.890
When she was arrested, thousands of people boycotted Montgomery buses. The city lost money, and segregation on buses was stopped the following year.

8
00:01:04.890 --> 00:01:12.890
Then there was the Little Rock Nine. Nine black teenagers were determined to attend an all-white school in Arkansas.

9
00:01:12.890 --> 00:01:23.890
On their first day of class in 1957, they were blocked by an angry mob of people. Two weeks later, the President of the United States sent soldiers to protect them.

10
00:01:23.890 --> 00:01:27.890
These soldiers remained at the school the entire year.

11
00:01:27.890 --> 00:01:33.890
The most recognizable figure of the Civil Rights Movement was Dr. Martin Luther King, Jr.

12
00:01:33.890 --> 00:01:42.890
Dr. King was known for organizing nonviolent protests, putting freedom riders on segregated buses, and arranging sit-ins at restaurants.

13
00:01:42.890 --> 00:01:54.890
In 1963, hundreds of thousands of people marched to Washington, D.C. to demand equality. There, Dr. King gave his famous I Have a Dream speech.

14
00:01:54.890 --> 00:02:02.890
The Civil Rights Act of 1964 was a huge victory for the movement. It made it illegal to discriminate against people based on their race.

15
00:02:02.890 --> 00:02:10.890
But it can take a long time for people's attitudes to catch up to the law. Even to this day, there's still work to be done.
